# 2. Runtime Data Areas

## Interview Answer

Runtime Data Areas are memory regions used by JVM during program execution.

Some areas are shared by all threads and some are thread-specific.

---

## Runtime Data Areas Diagram

```text
                 JVM Memory

       Shared By All Threads
       ---------------------
       Heap
       Metaspace
       Code Cache

       Per Thread
       ----------
       Stack
       PC Register
       Native Method Stack
```

---

# 2.1 Heap Memory

## Interview Answer

Heap is the memory area where all objects and instance variables are stored.

It is shared among all threads.

Garbage Collector primarily works on Heap memory.

---

## Example

```java
Employee emp = new Employee();
```

Object:

```java
new Employee()
```

is stored in Heap.

Reference:

```java
emp
```

is stored in Stack.

---

## Heap Structure

```text
Heap
 |
 +-- Young Generation
 |      |
 |      +-- Eden
 |      +-- Survivor S0
 |      +-- Survivor S1
 |
 +-- Old Generation
```

---

## Cross Question

### Q: Why do we get OutOfMemoryError?

Answer:

When Heap memory becomes full and JVM cannot allocate more memory.

```text
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
```

---

# 2.2 Stack Memory

## Interview Answer

Each thread has its own Stack memory.

Method calls, local variables, and references are stored in Stack.

---

## Example

```java
public void test() {
    int age = 25;
    Employee emp = new Employee();
}
```

Stack contains:

```text
age = 25
emp reference
```

Heap contains:

```text
Employee object
```

---

## Stack Frame

Every method call creates a Stack Frame.

```java
main()
   |
   +-- methodA()
           |
           +-- methodB()
```

Stack:

```text
methodB()
methodA()
main()
```

---

## Cross Question

### Q: Why StackOverflowError occurs?

Answer:

Due to excessive recursive calls.

Example:

```java
public void test() {
    test();
}
```

Error:

```text
java.lang.StackOverflowError
```

---

# 2.3 Metaspace

## Interview Answer

Metaspace stores class metadata.

Before Java 8 it was called PermGen.

From Java 8 onwards it was replaced by Metaspace.

---

## Stores

- Class definitions
- Method metadata
- Static metadata
- Runtime constant pool

---

## Example

```java
Employee.class
```

Class information is stored in Metaspace.

---

## Cross Question

### Q: Difference between PermGen and Metaspace?

| PermGen | Metaspace |
|----------|----------|
| Java 7 and below | Java 8+ |
| Fixed size | Dynamically grows |
| Part of JVM memory | Uses native memory |

---

# 2.4 Code Cache

## Interview Answer

Code Cache stores compiled native machine code generated by the JIT Compiler.

This improves performance because JVM doesn't need to interpret bytecode repeatedly.

---

## Flow

```text
Bytecode
    ↓
JIT Compiler
    ↓
Native Machine Code
    ↓
Code Cache
```

---

## Benefit

Frequently executed methods run faster.

---

# 3. Execution Engine

## Interview Answer

Execution Engine executes the bytecode loaded into memory.

It consists of:

1. Interpreter
2. JIT Compiler
3. Garbage Collector

---

# Interpreter

Reads bytecode line by line and executes it.

```text
Bytecode
   ↓
Interpreter
   ↓
Execution
```

---

## Problem

Slow because it translates every instruction repeatedly.

---

# JIT Compiler (Just-In-Time Compiler)

Compiles frequently used bytecode into native machine code.

```text
Bytecode
    ↓
JIT
    ↓
Machine Code
```

Next execution becomes much faster.

---

## Example

Frequently called method:

```java
calculateSalary()
```

gets compiled into machine code.

---

# Garbage Collector

## Interview Answer

Garbage Collector automatically removes unused objects from Heap memory.

---

## Example

```java
Employee emp = new Employee();

emp = null;
```

Object becomes eligible for garbage collection.

---

